+# DOT OIG's congressional list of open recommendations and its scope
+
+November 9, 2026 · B129 / PD02–PD04. The actual February 29, 2008 OIG letter and attachment under **Control No. 2008-036** define a selected set of open recommendations. The attachment's index identifies **65 recommendations across 28 FAA audit reports**. I located no entry for the 9/11 Commission referral **CC-2006-085 / 042R0122001** in a scoped OCR screen of the 58-page packet. The letter specifies what the OIG chose to report and points to an earlier criteria letter. The missing case-specific response and recommendation outcomes remain open. [Letter and attachment](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOTOIGOpenRxresponsefeb29.pdf) · [OIG library entry](https://www.oig.dot.gov/library-item/30573)
+
+## Preserve the scopes and counts side by side
+
+| Record | Period and population as reported |
+| --- | --- |
+| **February 29, 2008 OIG letter**, physical p. 1 | Describes the December 7, 2007 request as covering **January 1, 2001, to the present**. It says a **January 15, 2008** letter gave criteria for identifying key open recommendations from **January 1, 2001 to December 17, 2007**. Selection addresses significant safety, security or oversight issues, or financial benefits. It reports **168 in 81 DOT audit reports**, plus **four in one NTSB audit report**, and describes an attachment of **172**. [Letter, p. 1](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOTOIGOpenRxresponsefeb29.pdf#page=1) |
+| **Attachment index**, physical p. 3, printed p. 1 of 56 | Its face repeats **January 1, 2001 – December 17, 2007**. The FAA line is **65 / 28**. The ten DOT and shared-responsibility lines sum to **168 / 81**. Including the separate NTSB row gives **172 / 82**. These calculations check the index's counts against the cover, rather than independently counting every recommendation. [Index, p. 3](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOTOIGOpenRxresponsefeb29.pdf#page=3) |
+| **DOT OIG semiannual report**, physical p. 72 | Repeats the request's **to the present** scope and the **168 / 81** plus **four / one** counts. Its summary describes selection for significance or financial benefits, but gives no December 17 cutoff in this paragraph. The case attachment and the summary share OIG authorship. [Semiannual, p. 72](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/oigsar_0708.pdf#page=72) |
+| **House majority staff's January 2009 report**, physical pp. 5 and 15 | Describes the first request as **January 1, 2001–December 31, 2007**, followed by a **December 2008** request for an update through year-end. Its DOT row gives **339 open** and **2,241 made** for 2001–2008. The open counts by year are **3, 11, 11, 6, 13, 34, 69, 192**, giving **147** for 2001–2007 and **192** for 2008. The dated DOT update and row-level bridge to the earlier selected **168** remain unacquired. [Method, p. 5](https://oversight.house.gov/wp-content/uploads/2012/01/200901Majority_Report.pdf#page=5) · [Appendix, p. 15](https://oversight.house.gov/wp-content/uploads/2012/01/200901Majority_Report.pdf#page=15) |
+
+The original request and response criteria are different fields. The OIG and House descriptions use different cutoff language, and the two returns have different reporting dates and stated scope. Preserve **168**, **147**, and **339** with their own populations. The difference between **168** and **147** cannot be assigned to individual recommendations or counted as closures without the later underlying return and status history.
+
+## What the screen covered
+
+The response has two cover pages and 56 numbered attachment pages. All 58 page images were rendered at 180 dpi and OCR screened. All 56 attachment footers were found in order. Case-number, title, Commission question, bridge, NORAD and hijack-response locator checks yielded zero hits. Three positive controls located the cover control number on both pages, the first FAA audit **AV2003030** on physical p. 4, and the NTSB audit **QC2008009** on physical p. 58. The PDF has no embedded text. Selected cover, index, table and House-row images were visually checked. The complete original scans are retained separately. [Verification scope, source hashes and line locators](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+The FAA entries begin at physical p. 4 and continue through p. 23. Shared-responsibility entries begin at p. 54. A scan search can miss a row, another identifier or an unrecognized subject. The larger limitation is the defined population. This is a selected audit recommendation attachment. Whether the investigative referral's recommendations were considered for selection, tracked separately, implemented or left open remains unresolved. [First FAA table, p. 4](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOTOIGOpenRxresponsefeb29.pdf#page=4) · [Shared-responsibility table, p. 54](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOTOIGOpenRxresponsefeb29.pdf#page=54) · [Separate 9/11 referral, p. 12](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12)
+
